            "body": "> Thanks for the issue!\r\n> \r\n> This works as intended. The default `search_method` [is `'cover'`](https://github.com/echasnovski/mini.nvim/blob/e50cf9de614500a20e47cfc50e30a100042f91c3/doc/mini-surround.txt#L526) which means that surrounding is searched in a way that left part is before and right part is after the cursor (or more precisely: left part is before left edge of reference region and right part is after right edge).\r\n> \r\n> When called from the first line, it doesn't find any surrounding (as it mentions in the message below) and in the second line it correctly identifies last `\"` on previous line and first `\"` on current line as covering surrounding.\r\n> \r\n> To surround \"next\"/\"previous\" surrounding I'd recommend explicitly use `srn` / `srl` variants. Or set different default search method, like this: `require('mini.surround').setup({ search_method = 'cover_or_next' })`.\r\n> \r\n> Closing as everything working as expected.\r\n\r\nThis actually also happen with `cover_or_next`",
